1.1 Test analysis and Design
To reduce reduncancy, I would use both Equivalence Partitioning and Boundary Value Analysis to ensure that every field is mostly covered.
- Happy path:
    - Register will all valid details
    - Verify that the "Confirm Password" matches the "Password".
- Negative path:
    - Input different strings for "Confirm Password" and "Password"
    - Enter an email where First Name or Last Name is required.
    - Test whether the system allows duplicate usernames
- Special case or edge case:
    -Testing if the system allows Special characters where normal strings are required.

1.2. Prioritisation and strategy:
I would prioritise a positive and most critical negative sccenario first. 
To validate first that a user is able to Register with basic valid credentials before moving onto testing all the field validations after, 
which would after involve checking the spelling errors or clear error messages on the UI test.
For this I would use either Selenium or Cypress automated regression.

1.3. Client collaboration:
I think it's important to understand the requirements which might not be specified in the task, for example password requirements(length, complexity) in order to have some acceptance criteria in place.
Reporting: 
I would provide Test summary reports to the client at the end of the test cycle highlighting bugs found and coverage and qulity of the feature.
